A||||11||8Ball||Click but will not start PLEASE HELP!!||||4||
Z||000000||8Ball||11-15-2001||02:32 PM||||Ok My car has Died again at work.<BR>The other day this happened and I put in a new battery. I can hear it click but it does nothing else. Last time this happened I left it alone for a while and it started up no problems. But thus far it will not start this time. When it is working my starter seems to work great. I barely turn the key and she will start right up every time.<P>Any Ideas on what it could be. It is an 86 with a 2.8<BR>I have tried running it thru thr gears to make sure it is fully engaged in park. And still no luck.<P>Please help so I am not walking home from work.<P>Thanks,<BR>Randy Walker||||reg||4
Z||000001||JohnF||11-15-2001||02:38 PM||||A click without turning over the starter indicates insufficient amperage getting to the starter itself.<P>Insufficient amperage could be anything from the cable at the battery (probably + side) to the amperage being applied at the starter by the solenoid.<P>First, make very sure the cable is good and making good contact with the battery.  Then go from there. ||||reg||9
Z||000002||Voytek||11-15-2001||03:04 PM||||I'll second that.<P>I've had a similar problem once.  The connection on the battery was somewhat tight, but enough so I could still move the clamp by hand.  The car clicked but 'no go'.<P>If your battery is new/good and your wires are all good, including connections, I would move onto the starter.  The starter has an electromagnet that pulls the little gear along the shaft, in and out of the housing (the one that engages to your flywheel).  If the electromagnet is good, but the starter motor is shot, you'll hear the click but it won't turn over.<P>Good luck.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/voyteks_sig2.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||8Ball||11-15-2001||03:09 PM||||ok Here is an update.<BR>I went out and checked my wires. They seemed to be connected tightly so I smacked the starter with a tire iron a couple times then tried to start it. It started right up.<P>So do I need a new starter or was that a coincidence???<P>Thanks guys,<BR>Randy||||reg||1
Z||000004||JohnF||11-15-2001||03:16 PM||||I'm going to guess either <P>(1) a bad connection between the solenoid and the starter not allowing sufficient amperage or <P>(2) a burned/shorted/"flat" spot on the starter armature or <P>(3) bad brushes in the starter <P>(I'm going with #2, BTW!).||||reg||1
Z||000005||Voytek||11-15-2001||05:21 PM||||What John means is that you need a new starter.<P>Check one more thing - the connection on the starter itself (if you haven't done so).||||reg||1
Z||000006||8Ball||11-15-2001||06:02 PM||||Thanks guys.<BR>I will buy a new starter this weekend.<BR>At least it is fairly easy to get to.<BR>Thanks again,<BR>Randy Walker<P>You guys are great||||reg||1
Z||000007||JohnF||11-15-2001||06:22 PM||||Yeah, I guess I get a little too technical at times.  Thanks for reading between the lines Voytek.  <P>I just don't want to be the one that diagnoses a problem over the net, only to find out it was a loose nut behind the wheel.  Know what I mean?<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000008||reddog||11-15-2001||08:24 PM||||Hey 8Ball hows it going. Before you replace the starter check the connections! If you get a bunch of oil ,dirt,or whatever on the solenoid it could just be the connection needs cleaned off.Happened on my blazer once. If you do replace it try Advanced Auto they have a life time warranty if it ever fails they will replace it for free!! Other than the starter hows your car doing. I just bought another 86 GT 4 speed. it's purple. I bought it for $1500 and it's in great condition except for an exhaust leak!<P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Picturenew_sig.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000009||ray b||11-15-2001||08:45 PM||||quick fix on old chevy starters was to flip the coin[the coin shaped copper disk that make contact in the starter solenoid] and clean the contact it hits,a 5 min job no parts needed cost zero.<P>------------------<BR>Question wonder and be wierd||||reg||10
Z||000010||Tom88gt||11-15-2001||11:01 PM||||It might not be your starter.  If you smacked it and then it started, I'd guess that your STARTER SOLENOID is sticking.  You can replace it for less than half the cost of the starter.  But usually GM starters are sold with the solenoid already mounted.<P>Also, a single click is a strong indicator of a nearly dead battery.  Do a voltage check of your battery also.  If the voltage guage in the car bottoms out when you try to start your battery is dead.  A good battery will only register as a partial drop on the car's voltage guage when you are starting.<P>If you battey is dead, why?...a new starter won't solve the problem.||||reg||3
Z||000011||8Ball||11-16-2001||10:26 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by reddog:<BR><B>Hey 8Ball hows it going. Before you replace the starter check the connections! If you get a bunch of oil ,dirt,or whatever on the solenoid it could just be the connection needs cleaned off.Happened on my blazer once. If you do replace it try Advanced Auto they have a life time warranty if it ever fails they will replace it for free!! Other than the starter hows your car doing. I just bought another 86 GT 4 speed. it's purple. I bought it for $1500 and it's in great condition except for an exhaust leak!<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>||||reg||1
